  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An old woman with poor eyesight offers a boy an inedible meal, then warns him that if he wishes to keep his bundle of feathers he must walk around blooming flower fields. He obeys for a time but eventually enters the flowers, where the feathers release living wings and vanish. The narrator explains that these become the little birds that bring summer and hover over flowers, and closes with a reflection on Zuni oral knowledge as words carried in the heart.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The old woman says she has been gathering cactus-fruit, but the pile contains mostly cedar-berries, cones, sticks, dirt, and other uneatable things.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The old woman tells the boy to go home and instructs him to walk around any blooming flowers or sunflower fields if he wants to keep the feathers.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2",
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The boy walks around several flower patches, then decides he will not keep doing so because there seem to be flowers all the way home.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "When the boy enters the flowers holding the bundle, little wings fly out and the bundle of feathers disappears.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The narrator identifies the released wings as those of the Sacred Birds of Summerland and says they become living in the flower-filled summer environment.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The narrator says this is why there are little birds that bring the summer and hover over flowers.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The story-teller says the Zuñis also have books, described as words placed in their hearts by the ancients when the world was new.",
