    "label": "CHAPTER II. / IDUN AND HER APPLES. / CHAPTER III. / HOW NJORD GOT SKADE TO WIFE.; lines 3454-3484",
    "start": "3454",
    "end": "3484",
    "translation": "The Younger Edda; Also called Snorre's Edda, or The Prose Edda",
    "notes": "Stable markdown line range generated from canonical text."
  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Skade comes armed to Asgard to avenge Thjasse, but the gods settle with her by giving atonement terms: she may choose a husband by seeing only the gods' feet, and they must make her laugh. She chooses Njord by mistake, Loki makes her laugh through a goat-tugging performance, Odin makes stars from Thjasse's eyes, and Brage explains poetic names for gold through the inheritance of Olvalde's sons.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Skade, daughter of the giant Thjasse, puts on helmet, byrnie, and war-gear and goes to Asgard to avenge her father's death.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The asas offer Skade ransom and atonement.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "One settlement term is that Skade may choose a husband among the asas by seeing only their feet.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Skade chooses a man with beautiful feet, thinks she has chosen Balder, but the man is Njord from Noatun.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Another settlement term is that the asas must do something Skade thinks they cannot do, namely make her laugh.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Loki ties one end of a string to a goat's beard and the other around his own body, and he and the goat pull against each other while shrieking.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Loki falls on Skade's knees, and this makes her laugh.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Odin takes Thjasse's eyes, casts them into heaven, and makes two stars from them.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Brage says that Thjasse's father was Olvalde, who was very rich in gold.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Olvalde's sons divide the inheritance by equal mouthfuls of gold, and Brage says that in runes and songs gold is called the mouth-number, measure, word, or tale of these giants.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6",
